{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>To the chief musician, to Jeduthun</b> — I already mentioned at the beginning of the book. And in my opinion, there was a known melody in the south, and Jeduthun composed it, or another composed it and praised him, and it was the chief melody for Jeduthun. This psalm too is like the one above, for it speaks of his illness." "<b>The meaning of 'I said'</b> — in my heart." "<b>The meaning of 'I will guard my ways'</b> — my ways and my conduct, like 'and all my ways you have searched' [Psalm 139:3]. The meaning is that I will not speak, as a sick man speaks out of his great distress, and as though there is a muzzle in my mouth — 'I will guard it' being in my mouth, and I will not remove it."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {0 "The phrase 'at the beginning of the book' likely refers to his commentary on Psalm 4 (the first occurrence of למנצח in the Psalms); resolved by inference."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:30:15.756275934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}